.TH SDE-LOCK 1 "October 2026" "SDE" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sde-lock \- SDE Screen Lock
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sde-lock
[\fB\-\-lock\fR]
.br
.B sde-lock
\fB\-\-daemon\fR | \fB\-\-query\fR | \fB\-\-poke\fR | \fB\-\-inhibitors\fR | \fB\-\-version\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Without options (or with \fB\-\-lock\fR) the screen is locked now; the
command returns 0 once the keyboard and pointer are grabbed and fails when
they could not be (the screen is then \fBnot\fR locked).
.PP
\fBsde-lock \-\-daemon\fR is the session service (XDG autostart). It
implements org.freedesktop.ScreenSaver (Lock, GetActive, SetActive,
Inhibit, UnInhibit, ...) and org.debion.SDE.ScreenLock, locks after
[lock] idle_lock seconds unless a program inhibits idleness, locks before
suspend (logind PrepareForSleep, with a delay inhibitor) and follows
logind's Lock and Unlock requests (\fBloginctl lock-session\fR).
.PP
The lock screen runs in its own process. If it ends without a correct
password, the screen stays black and grabbed and it is started again.
Passwords are checked by PAM with the service file /etc/pam.d/sde-lock;
wrong passwords delay further attempts.
.TP
.B \-\-query
Print "locked" or "unlocked".
.TP
.B \-\-poke
Simulate user activity (turns the monitors back on).
.TP
.B \-\-inhibitors
List the programs that keep the session from idling.
.PP
Settings: \fBsde-settings \-\-page lock\fR, or [lock] in
~/.config/sde/sde.conf. \fBsde-screensaver-command\fR remains as a
compatibility wrapper.
.SH FILES
/etc/pam.d/sde-lock
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sde-power (1),
.BR loginctl (1)
